// #88, review round 3: a walked expression keeps its type, so a binding made
// from a value the walk of a generic body recovered is still made — and the
// comparison after it is refused by name.
//
// The walk enforces the reference rules only, and `return 7` at `-> N` (an
// alias of `u32`, told apart from `Int` there) is refused for another reason.
// The value block holding it was walked part by part but answered no type, so
// `let u = { .. &a }` bound nothing, `u == w` read an undefined name, and the
// comparison of two borrowed fives went unjudged. Measured on main (2ca5eb5)
// and on round 3 alike: it compiled, ran and printed `ne`, then `7`. The
// block's type is recovered from its value, and `u` is bound.
type N = u32;

fn g<T>(a: T, b: T) -> N {
    let u = {
        if false {
            return 7;
        }
        &a
    };
    let w = &b;
    if u == w {
        print("eq");
    } else {
        print("ne");
    }
    7
}

fn main() {
    print_int(g(5, 5));
}
